rules_version = '2';

// ⚠️ REFERENCE ONLY. DO NOT `firebase deploy --only firestore:rules` THIS FILE.
//
// The bot's actual Firebase project (tournament-tracker-f35tb) is shared with
// the Tournament Tracker app, which owns a much larger, actively-maintained
// ruleset covering teams/matches/standins/fantasy lineups/admin roles/the bot
// orchestrator's own collections (botAccounts, botCommands, botEvents,
// botLobbySessions, botSyncTasks — the Next.js "orchestrator" side of the same
// worker-api.md contract the Discord gateway uses). Deploying this file would
// REPLACE that entire ruleset with the single deny-all line below — there is
// no rules file for this repo to own or deploy.
//
// Verified 2026-08-08 by reading the live ruleset: it ends in
//   match /{document=**} { allow read, write: if false; }
// and nothing above that catch-all names any `inhouse*` collection. Every
// inhouseGames / inhousePlayers / inhouseAttendance / inhouseModeration /
// inhouseBans / inhouseConfig / inhouseLinkCodes / inhouseReadyPool /
// inhouseMatches / inhouseCounters document is therefore ALREADY covered by
// that trailing deny-all, with zero action needed on our side. This file is
// kept only as documentation of what protection level those collections need
// — which the live rules already provide — should anyone ever need to verify
// that by inspection rather than re-reading the other repo's ruleset.
//
// The website never touches Firestore from the browser anyway (§2.2) — every
// read/write goes through the Admin SDK server-side, which bypasses rules
// entirely. So even a maximally permissive live ruleset for the inhouse
// collections would not be a functional bug for us — it would only be a
// security gap, and per the verification above, that gap doesn't exist.
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
